{
  "id": "04-largest-rectangle-histogram",
  "title": "Largest Rectangle in Histogram",
  "track": "Stacks",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "topics": [
    "stacks",
    "arrays"
  ],
  "source": "canonical Largest Rectangle in Histogram concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The input is an array of non-negative integers heights, where heights[i] is the height of the bar at position i and every bar is 1 unit wide. A rectangle spans consecutive bars and rises only as high as the shortest bar it covers. Return the largest rectangle area that fits inside the histogram.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(heights) <= 5000",
    "0 <= heights[i] <= 10000"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    "heights"
  ],
  "functionName": "largest_rectangle_area",
  "starterCode": "def largest_rectangle_area(heights):\n    # Return the largest rectangle area under the histogram.\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": {
        "heights": [
          3,
          1,
          3,
          2,
          2
        ]
      },
      "output": 6,
      "explanation": "The rectangle over positions 2..4 has height 2 and width 3, for an area of 6."
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "heights": [
          5
        ]
      },
      "output": 5,
      "explanation": "A single bar of height 5 fits a 5 x 1 rectangle."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The widest rectangle at height h stretches from just after the nearest shorter bar on the left to just before the nearest shorter bar on the right.",
    "When a bar is shorter than the one before it, the taller bars before it can never extend past this point: their best rectangles are now fully determined.",
    "Keep a stack of bars with increasing heights. When the current bar is lower, pop and finish each popped bar’s rectangle using the stack top as its left boundary; flush the rest at the end."
  ],
  "solution": "Monotonic increasing stack of indices. When the current bar is shorter than the stack top, pop and compute the popped bar’s maximal rectangle: height times the width between the new stack top and the current index. Every bar is pushed and popped once: O(n) time, O(n) space.",
  "solutionCode": "def largest_rectangle_area(heights):\n    stack = []\n    best = 0\n    for i, h in enumerate(heights + [0]):\n        while stack and heights[stack[-1]] >= h:\n            top = stack.pop()\n            left = stack[-1] if stack else -1\n            width = i - left - 1\n            area = heights[top] * width\n            if area > best:\n                best = area\n        if i < len(heights):\n            stack.append(i)\n    return best\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def largest_rectangle_area(heights):\n    best = 0\n    for i in range(len(heights)):\n        lowest = heights[i]\n        for j in range(i, len(heights)):\n            if heights[j] < lowest:\n                lowest = heights[j]\n            area = lowest * (j - i + 1)\n            if area > best:\n                best = area\n    return best\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include zeros, equal bars, a tall bar at each end, and plateaus",
  "expectedComplexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
